  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Showers and storms around today, especially towards \nthe CT River Valley where an isolated strong to severe thunderstorm \nor two is possible.\n\nWeak low pressure just off to our northeast continues to aid in \ncontinued NE low level flow this morning, keeping overcast skies \nover southern New England. Chances for spotty showers continue \nprimarily towards Nantucket and the outer Cape where showers are \njust offshore and continuing to move off to the northeast. Aside \nfrom these, misty and foggier conditions continue this morning \nacross the region.\n\nThe main thing to keep an eye out for today will be the chances for \nsome showers and storms towards the afternoon. Showers tracking \nacross New York associated with the approaching cold front will \napproach western MA and CT towards the late morning/early afternoon \nhours. The low pressure to our northeast loosens its grip over \nsouthern New England as more southerly winds take hold in the late \nmorning/early afternoon hours, advecting in dewpoints in the 70s. \nIncreased humidity along with diurnal instability may enhance the \nrisk for storms in the interior turning strong to severe. Even with \ncloudier skies inhibiting instability a bit, model soundings and CAM \nguidance have MUCAPE values around 1500-1800 J/kg over the CT Valley \nin the afternoon, and MLCAPE values to around 1200-1300 J/kg with \nlimited capping in the soundings. Effective bulk shear values around \n40 kt will also aid in development of an isolated stronger to severe \nstorm or two, with the main risk of these storms being damaging \nwinds. PWAT values still around 1.25-1.5\" in the afternoon ahead of \nthe front will also keep the risk for some torrential downpours in \nplay. The interior remains in a Marginal (1/5) risk for severe \nweather today considering this environment.\n\nOutside of this risk of showers and storms, the rest of the region \ncan expect to remain mostly dry, albeit dreary. Humid conditions \nprevail with highs in the upper 70s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Drier and warmer next week before rain chances make \na return sometime Thursday/Friday.\n\nThe cold front to our west passes through the region heading into \nMonday, clearing skies quickly in its wake. Drier post-frontal air \nflows into the region, noted by dew points falling into the 50s and \nlow 60s and PWATs to an inch and lower. Temperatures return to more \nseasonable levels in the 70s and 80s, though it should feel more \ncomfortable than today given the reduced humidity. High pressure \nsteadily builds in through Tuesday into Wednesday as ridging takes \nplace of the troughing from Quebec that will continue to shift off \nto the east. Clear skies and dry weather prevail through this period.\n\nGreater uncertainty reenters the forecast towards Thursday and \nFriday with the approach and development of another shortwave over \nthe Great Lakes. Ensemble and deterministic guidance does hint at \nthe return of elevated moisture towards this time period, along with \nrain showers. Latest ensemble guidance has PWAT values returning to \naround 1.5\" Thursday afternoon into Friday, along with more \nsoutherly flow towards the surface. Inconsistencies remain among the \nensembles, though, regarding the track of the low associated with \nthe shortwave. This also leads to discrepancies regarding the timing \nof any showers.",
